*  CN / an UNDECLARED &name read inside an EVAL-compiled fragment.  The fragment never folds, so
*  the read reaches the cell, finds no binding, and raises 342 -- and 342 inside EVAL is now a
*  STATEMENT FAILURE, not the death of the program.
*  ⛔ THIS FILE WAS `.err_sno` UNTIL THIS SEAT AND ITS PROMOTION IS THE POINT.  s153 minted it as
*  an abort lane and said so in as many words -- "This witness pins TODAY'S behaviour in both
*  media so KW-5 moves it deliberately rather than silently" -- and the CN-4 gate carried the
*  matching assertion `grep 342 <stderr>`.  Lon's s168 oracle directive ruled the open question
*  (manual v3.7 p.131, "EVAL fails if evaluation of its argument fails"): live sbl FAILS and runs
*  on, so SCRIP does too, and the visible gate event this pin existed to produce has now fired.
*  ⛔ THE .ref IS ORACLE-LAW-DERIVED, NOT AN ORACLE RUN, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS DELIBERATE:
*  &USER_DECLARED_CONSTANTS is a SCRIP extension with no sbl counterpart, so this exact program
*  cannot be run on the oracle at all.  The CONTROL FLOW it pins -- EVAL fails, &ERRTEXT is left
*  set, the program reaches its end -- is the half that IS oracle-run, byte-for-byte, in the
*  sibling witness cn_eval_fails_not_aborts.{sno,ref}.  Read the two together: that one owns the
*  oracle receipt, this one owns the UDC-armed reach.
	&USER_DECLARED_CONSTANTS = 1
	&N = 42
	OUTPUT = 'before=' &N
	X = EVAL('&NEVERSET')	:F(F1)
	OUTPUT = 'eval-OK=[' X ']'	:(S1)
F1	OUTPUT = 'eval-FAILED'
	DIFFER(&ERRTEXT)	:F(S1)
	OUTPUT = 'errtext-nonnull'
S1	OUTPUT = 'after=' &N
	OUTPUT = 'reached-end'
END
